In the project at `/tmp/sv-266193681302479ca438fc571e459e8e/before/`, I need to fix the `ManagedServiceIdentity.Serialization.cs` file and the `ArmEnvironment.cs` file.

## Fix 1: ManagedServiceIdentity.Serialization.cs

File: `Common/Custom/Models/ManagedServiceIdentity.Serialization.cs`

This file has a `Write` method that uses `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, ManagedServiceIdentityType, jOptions)` at line 31 and `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, item.Value, jOptions)` at line 49.

The `ManagedServiceIdentityType` struct has a `[JsonConverter(typeof(ManagedServiceIdentityTypeConverter))]` attribute and the converter writes `"type"` property name then the string value. Looking at `ManagedServiceIdentityTypeConverter.Write()`:
```csharp
public override void Write(Utf8JsonWriter writer, ManagedServiceIdentityType model, JsonSerializerOptions options)
{
    writer.WritePropertyName("type");
    writer.WriteStringValue(model.ToString());
}
```

So `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, ManagedServiceIdentityType, jOptions)` writes `"type": "value"`. Replace it with the equivalent inline:
```csharp
writer.WritePropertyName("type"u8);
writer.WriteStringValue(ManagedServiceIdentityType.ToString());
```

For `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, item.Value, jOptions)` at line 49 where `item.Value` is `UserAssignedIdentity` which implements `IJsonModel<UserAssignedIdentity>`, replace with:
```csharp
writer.WriteObjectValue(item.Value, ModelSerializationExtensions.WireOptions);
```

Also, the deserialization at line 206:
```csharp
type = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ManagedServiceIdentityType>($"{{{property}}}");
```
The `ManagedServiceIdentityTypeConverter.Read()` parses a JSON object like `{"type": "value"}` and returns the type from the "type" property. But `$"{{{property}}}"` creates a string like `{"type":"SystemAssigned"}` from the property. So this is just extracting the string value. Replace with:
```csharp
type = new ManagedServiceIdentityType(property.Value.GetString());
```

And line 218:
```csharp
dictionary.Add(new ResourceIdentifier(property0.Name), JsonSerializer.Deserialize<UserAssignedIdentity>(property0.Value.GetRawText()));
```
Replace with:
```csharp
dictionary.Add(new ResourceIdentifier(property0.Name), UserAssignedIdentity.DeserializeUserAssignedIdentity(property0.Value));
```

Also, the `Write` method signature has `JsonSerializerOptions jOptions = null` parameter which is no longer needed since we're not using JsonSerializer. But since the method is `internal` and called from line 58 (`Write(writer, options, null)`), we should keep the signature but the parameter becomes unused. That's fine—just remove the usage.

Make these 4 edits in the file.

## Fix 2: ArmEnvironment.cs

File: `ArmEnvironment.cs`

Line 80: `public override string ToString() => JsonSerializer.Serialize(this);`

Since `ArmEnvironment` is a simple struct with `Endpoint` (Uri) and `Audience` (string), replace with a manual JSON construction that's AOT-safe:
```csharp
public override string ToString()
{
    return $"{{\"Endpoint\":\"{Endpoint}\",\"Audience\":\"{Audience}\",\"DefaultScope\":\"{DefaultScope}\"}}";
}
```

Wait, that's not proper JSON escaping. Better to use Utf8JsonWriter:
```csharp
public override string ToString()
{
    var buffer = new System.Buffers.ArrayBufferWriter<byte>();
    using var writer = new Utf8JsonWriter(buffer);
    writer.WriteStartObject();
    writer.WriteString("Endpoint", Endpoint.AbsoluteUri);
    writer.WriteString("Audience", Audience);
    writer.WriteString("DefaultScope", DefaultScope);
    writer.WriteEndObject();
    writer.Flush();
    return System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetString(buffer.WrittenSpan);
}
```

Actually this is simpler and AOT-safe. Make this change. You'll need to add `using System.Buffers;` and `using System.Text;` if not already present (check imports at top of file). The file already has `using System.Text.Json;` so Utf8JsonWriter is available.

Make all the edits.

